# What is a thread?

A **thread** is one sequence of execution. Want two things at once — serve two requests, use eight cores — you need more than one, and you enter **concurrency**.

## One process, many threads

```text
Process (one JVM)
├── shared heap: objects, static fields  ← all threads see these
├── Thread "main"     — own stack + program counter
└── Thread "worker-1" — own stack
```

Private stacks, **one shared heap** — the source of concurrency's power *and* its every hazard.

## Concurrency ≠ parallelism

- **Concurrency** = *structure* — many tasks in overlapping time (interleaved, even on 1 core)
- **Parallelism** = *execution* — literally at once (needs multiple cores)

## Why threads — and why they've been hard

- **Responsiveness** — don't freeze while waiting · **Throughput** — use every core
- **Expensive** — a classic thread = 1 OS thread ≈ 1 MB → only *thousands*
- **Dangerous** — the shared heap corrupts under uncoordinated access

**Java 21's virtual threads** fix the cost; **locks & the memory model** fix the danger.
